      title: "Presidential Reconstruction",
      body: `## Lincoln's plans

Reconstruction had begun during the war. In December 1863 Lincoln announced a lenient plan: when 10 percent of a state's voters of 1860 took an oath of loyalty to the Union and accepted emancipation, they could form a new state government. Most Confederates would receive amnesty. The "Ten Percent Plan" was designed to encourage Southern Unionists and to weaken the Confederacy. Radical Republicans in Congress thought it far too lenient. In July 1864 they passed the **Wade-Davis Bill**, which required a majority of voters to take an "ironclad oath" that they had never supported the Confederacy. Lincoln pocket-vetoed it. At his death, he had not settled on a final policy, though, as you saw in Lesson III, he had begun to endorse limited Black suffrage.

## Andrew Johnson

Lincoln's death made **Andrew Johnson** president. Johnson was a self-made man from Tennessee who had been born in poverty in North Carolina, apprenticed as a tailor, and largely self-taught; his wife, Eliza, helped him improve his writing. As a Democratic politician, he had championed small farmers against the planter aristocracy, which he hated. He was the only senator from a seceding state to remain loyal to the Union, and Lincoln had appointed him military governor of Tennessee and chosen him as running mate in 1864 to broaden the ticket's appeal. Radicals initially welcomed him; he had said that "treason must be made odious, and traitors must be punished."

But Johnson's hatred of the planters did not extend to sympathy for the people they had enslaved. He was a fervent believer in white supremacy, and a strict constructionist who believed that the states, not the federal government, should determine who could vote. "This is a country for white men," he reportedly declared in 1866, "and by God, as long as I am President, it shall be a government for white men."

## Johnson's plan

In May 1865, with Congress not in session until December, Johnson announced his own plan. He offered amnesty and restoration of property, except for enslaved people, to most Confederates who took an oath of allegiance. Wealthy Confederates—those with property worth more than $20,000—and high Confederate officials had to apply to him personally for pardons. Johnson appointed provisional governors, who would call conventions to write new state constitutions. The states had to abolish slavery, repudiate the Confederate debt, and nullify their ordinances of secession. They did not have to grant any rights to freedpeople.

Many Republicans expected Johnson to punish the planters. Instead, he granted thousands of pardons to wealthy Confederates who came to Washington to ask for them, apparently relishing their humility before him. The new state governments were controlled by former Confederates. Some conventions refused to repudiate the Confederate debt or merely "repealed" rather than nullified secession. When Congress assembled in December 1865, among the members elected by the restored Southern states were the former Confederate vice president, Alexander Stephens, four Confederate generals, and dozens of other Confederate officials. Congress refused to seat them.

## The Black Codes

The new Southern legislatures quickly passed laws known as the **Black Codes**, designed to define the status of freedpeople and to restore planter control over Black labor. The codes recognized some rights: freedpeople could marry, own some property, make contracts, and testify in court in cases involving Black people. But they also required Black people to sign annual labor contracts; those without contracts could be arrested as vagrants, fined, and, if unable to pay, hired out to employers who paid the fine. Mississippi's code forbade Black people to rent or lease land outside towns. South Carolina's required Black people who were not farmers or servants to pay a heavy annual tax. Apprenticeship laws allowed courts to bind out Black children to white employers, often their former owners, without their parents' consent. Laws punished "insulting" gestures, absence from work, and the breaking of contracts.

To many Northerners, the Black Codes seemed an attempt to restore slavery in all but name. Coming so soon after a war that had cost hundreds of thousands of Northern lives, they convinced many Republicans that the South had not accepted the results of the war.

## The Freedmen's Bureau

Standing between freedpeople and their former masters was the **Bureau of Refugees, Freedmen, and Abandoned Lands**, created by Congress in March 1865 and headed by General **Oliver O. Howard**, a devout Christian known as the "Christian General." The Freedmen's Bureau was the first federal social-welfare agency in American history. It distributed food and medical care to freedpeople and white refugees, established schools, supervised labor contracts between freedpeople and planters, and set up courts to hear disputes. It worked with Northern missionary societies to found schools, and by 1870 some 4,000 schools served about a quarter of a million students.

The Bureau was understaffed, with never more than about 900 agents for the entire South, and its agents varied enormously. Some were dedicated defenders of freedpeople's rights; others sided with planters, urging freedpeople to sign contracts and return to work on the plantations. The Bureau's central goal was to create a free-labor system in which freedpeople worked for wages under fair contracts. Its agents often found themselves pushing freedpeople back into plantation labor.

## The land reversed

The Bureau also controlled some 850,000 acres of abandoned and confiscated land, which Congress had authorized it to rent and eventually sell to freedpeople in forty-acre plots. But Johnson's pardons restored property to pardoned Confederates, and in the fall of 1865 he ordered the land returned to its former owners. Howard himself was sent to the Sea Islands to tell the freedpeople settled under Sherman's Field Order No. 15 that they must give up their land. The freedpeople protested. "You ask us to forgive the landholders of our island," a committee wrote to Howard. "You only lost your right arm in war and might forgive them. The man who tied me to a tree and gave me 39 lashes...that man, I cannot well forgive." Most were evicted.

## Violence

Violence against freedpeople was widespread. In May 1866, white mobs, including police, rampaged through Black neighborhoods in **Memphis** for three days, killing forty-six Black people and burning churches and schools. In July 1866, in **New Orleans**, police and white mobs attacked a convention of Black and white Republicans seeking Black suffrage, killing thirty-four Black people and three white supporters. To Northerners, these riots, along with the Black Codes, proved that Johnson's policy had failed.

> **Pause and consider.** Johnson hated the planter aristocracy. Why, then, did his policies restore the planters to power?`,
