      title: "Biracial Democracy in the South",
      body: `## A political revolution

Under the Reconstruction Acts, Black men in the South voted for the first time in 1867. About 700,000 Black men registered, outnumbering white voters in several states. In conventions across the South, Black and white delegates wrote new state constitutions—among the most democratic in the nation's history. They established public school systems, which the South had largely lacked before the war; abolished property qualifications for voting and office; reformed penal codes; and expanded the rights of women to own property.

## Black officeholders

For the first time, Black men held public office in significant numbers. Over the course of Reconstruction, about 2,000 Black men held office, from justice of the peace and sheriff to legislator and lieutenant governor. In South Carolina, Black men formed a majority of the lower house of the legislature from 1868 to 1876. Sixteen Black men served in Congress during Reconstruction. In 1870, **Hiram Revels** of Mississippi, a minister and educator, became the first Black senator, taking the seat once held by Jefferson Davis. **Blanche K. Bruce**, also of Mississippi, became the first Black senator to serve a full term. **Robert Smalls**, who had escaped slavery in 1862 by commandeering a Confederate steamer and delivering it to the Union navy, served five terms in the House from South Carolina.

![A group portrait of seven Black members of Congress in formal dress.](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/First_Colored_Senator_and_Representatives.jpg "Currier & Ives, *The First Colored Senator and Representatives in the 41st and 42nd Congress* (1872): Hiram Revels with Benjamin Turner, Robert DeLarge, Josiah Walls, Jefferson Long, Joseph Rainey, and Robert Brown Elliott. Library of Congress.")

Black officeholders were a diverse group. Many had been free before the war, often artisans, ministers, or teachers; many had served in the Union army; some were well educated, others barely literate. They did not dominate any state government; whites held most offices everywhere. But their presence marked a revolution. Men who had been enslaved a few years before now sat in legislatures that had once passed slave codes, debated railroad bonds and school funds, and wrote laws for their former masters. Their speeches, preserved in legislative records and newspapers, show them to have been, for the most part, moderate men who sought equal rights and public schools rather than revenge, and who often supported amnesty for former Confederates. Some, such as Francis Cardozo, South Carolina's secretary of state and later treasurer, had been educated at universities in Scotland and England; others, like Abraham Galloway of North Carolina, had escaped slavery and served as Union spies. The historian Eric Foner compiled the biographies of some 1,500 of them in *Freedom's Lawmakers* (1993).

## Carpetbaggers and scalawags

The Republican coalition in the South had three parts. Black voters, overwhelmingly, were the largest. Northerners who moved South after the war, whom their opponents derided as "**carpetbaggers**," supposedly arriving with all their belongings in a cheap carpet bag to exploit the South, were a second group. In fact, most were Union veterans, teachers, Freedmen's Bureau agents, or businessmen who had come to invest, and many genuinely sought to build a new South. White Southern Republicans, denounced as "**scalawags**," were the third. Many were former Whigs or Unionists, often small farmers from the upcountry who had opposed secession and resented the planters; others were planters and businessmen who hoped that cooperation with the Republicans would bring economic development.

## Achievements

The Reconstruction governments accomplished a great deal. They created the South's first state-funded public school systems, for both Black and white children, though almost always segregated. They built hospitals, asylums, and orphanages. They rebuilt roads and bridges and funded railroads. They reformed tax systems, shifting the burden from the poor to landowners. They passed civil rights laws forbidding discrimination in public accommodations. And they gave Black Southerners a voice in government that they would not have again until the 1960s.

## Corruption and debt

The Reconstruction governments were also accused of corruption and extravagance. Some officials took bribes, especially from railroads seeking state aid, and state debts rose sharply. Their enemies made these charges the centerpiece of attacks on Reconstruction, portraying Black officeholders as ignorant and corrupt. But corruption was a national phenomenon in the postwar years, from the Tweed Ring in New York to the railroad scandals of the Grant administration in Washington. Southern corruption was no worse than Northern, and Black officeholders were no more corrupt than white ones. Much of the rise in debt came from building schools and public institutions that had not existed before, and from the costs of rebuilding a war-ravaged region. Redeemer governments that replaced Reconstruction would be at least as corrupt.

## Political mobilization

Black political participation went beyond voting and officeholding. Freedpeople organized **Union Leagues**, political clubs that met in churches and schools to discuss politics, educate voters, and organize for elections. Political meetings drew entire communities. Black women, though they could not vote, attended conventions and rallies, debated political questions, and pressured men to vote for the Republicans; the historian Elsa Barkley Brown showed how, in Richmond, Black women understood the vote as a collective possession of the community. Black ministers became political leaders, and churches served as political headquarters.

![An engraving of Black men lining up to cast ballots: a workman in front, a well-dressed man behind him, and a soldier in uniform.](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/%22The_first_vote%22_-_AW_%28monogram%29_%3B_drawn_by_A.R._Waud._LCCN00651117.jpg "Alfred R. Waud, *The First Vote*, *Harper's Weekly*, November 16, 1867. The line of voters, an artisan, a professional man, and a Union soldier, made an argument for who had earned the ballot. Library of Congress.")

## The white response

Most white Southerners regarded the Reconstruction governments as illegitimate, imposed by Northern bayonets and Black votes. They called the Reconstruction state governments "**Black Republican**" or "Negro rule," though Black men held a majority of offices in no state. The Democratic Party organized to "redeem" the South from Republican rule. And from the start, white Southerners used violence to intimidate Black voters and their white allies. In Pulaski, Tennessee, around the turn of 1866, six Confederate veterans founded a secret society they called the **Ku Klux Klan**. It would become, as you will see in Lesson V, the instrument of a campaign of terror. The struggle for the South would be fought not only at the polls and in the legislatures, but in the countryside at night.

> **Pause and consider.** Why did the enemies of Reconstruction focus so heavily on corruption? Was the charge fair?`,
